<p>The sequence that wastes the least effort is: clear and protect, clean, fill
and repair, sand, caulk, spot-prime, then mask last. Doing them out of order
mostly means doing some of them twice &mdash; sanding before filling, or masking
before sanding, both guarantee a second pass.</p>

<h2>1. Clear the room and protect what stays</h2>

<p>Get furniture out if you can and into the middle under a sheet if you
cannot. Take down switch and outlet plates rather than cutting around them;
it takes two minutes and it is the difference between a crisp job and an obvious
one. Drop cloths on the floor: canvas grips and absorbs, plastic is cheaper but
slides underfoot and lets spills travel.</p>

<h2>2. Clean the walls, properly</h2>

<p>Paint sticks to the surface it touches, and if that surface is a film of
kitchen grease or cigarette residue, that is what it sticks to. Wash walls with
a degreasing cleaner and rinse; kitchens and any room where someone smoked need
it most. Let everything dry fully &mdash; trapping moisture under a fresh film
causes blistering weeks later.</p>

<p>Mildew is a special case. It has to be killed, not painted over, or it grows
through the new coat. Treat it, rinse it, and let it dry before priming.</p>

<h2>3. Fill and repair</h2>

<p>Lightweight spackle handles nail holes and small dings and sands easily.
Anything deeper than about a quarter inch wants joint compound built up in thin
coats rather than one thick one, because thick fills shrink and crack as they
cure. Cracks that keep reopening are usually structural movement, and taping the
crack with mesh or paper before filling is what stops it coming back through
next season.</p>

<h2>4. Sand, and get the dust off</h2>

<p>Sanding does two jobs: it levels your fills, and it dulls any glossy existing
paint so the new coat has something to grip. A medium grit knocks down repairs,
a fine grit finishes them. On glossy trim, scuff-sanding the whole surface
matters more than it looks like it should.</p>

<p>Then remove the dust. Vacuum, then wipe with a damp cloth, and let it dry.
Paint applied over sanding dust picks it up into the film and you will feel it
for the life of the wall.</p>

{{ img("dept-paint-spray-01.webp", imagemeta, "guide-figure", "lazy", "wide", derivatives) }}

<h2>5. Caulk the gaps</h2>

<p>A bead of paintable acrylic latex caulk where trim meets wall is the single
cheapest upgrade to how a room reads when finished. It closes the shadow line
that makes trim look separate from the wall. Use paintable caulk &mdash; pure
silicone will not take paint and you cannot fix that afterwards. Smooth the bead
with a wet finger and let it skin over before painting.</p>

<h2>6. Prime what needs it</h2>

<p>You do not always need to prime a whole room, but you do need to prime:
bare drywall, bare wood, anything you filled, anything glossy, and any stain
that would otherwise bleed through. Water stains, smoke and tannin-rich woods
need a stain-blocking primer specifically, because ordinary primer lets them
ghost back through the topcoat, sometimes weeks later.</p>

<p>Spot-priming repairs matters more than people expect. Filler is far more
absorbent than the surrounding painted wall, so an unprimed patch drinks the
topcoat and dries duller &mdash; the repair stays visible through two coats.
That effect is called flashing, and priming the patch is the whole fix.</p>

<h2>7. Mask last</h2>

<p>Tape goes on after sanding and cleaning, not before, or you will be taping
over dust and doing it again. Press the edge down firmly, and pull it while the
paint is still slightly soft rather than after it has fully hardened, so the
film parts cleanly instead of tearing.</p>

